    "OtherAbstract": {
      "Type": "PIP",
      "Language": "eng",
      "AbstractText": "The capability of opioid receptor agonists to reduce postoperative sterilization pain was investigated in 30 women who underwent postpartum tubal ligation (Pomeroy method) at Tampere (Finland) University Hospital. Using each patient as her own control eliminated inter-subject bias. One side of the fallopian tube was infiltrated with 5 mg of sufentanil and the contralateral side was infiltrated with physiologic saline. The results confirmed the importance of inflammation in the development of the peripheral antinociceptive action of opioid receptor agonists. The analgesic effect of sufentanil was not yet evident in some patients 15 minutes after the end of anesthesia administration. However, after 30 minutes from the end of anesthesia, 26 out of 30 women reported significant pain relief on the side of the sufentanil infiltration. In 11 of these women, pain relief lasted for at least 24 hours."
    }
  },
